# PATCH: 007-ramdisk-profile  —  NOT IMPLEMENTED (intentional)
#
# Decision date: 2026-05-14
# Decision:      Skip the RAM-disk profile patch. PBM-forced + locked disk-cache
#                prefs + SanitizeOnShutdown already keep page content off disk,
#                and a RAM disk does not solve the residual OS-level risks.
#                Full-disk encryption is required at the deployment layer
#                instead (documented in README.md).
#
# ── Why this patch is not implemented ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The driving concern was: "is there residual image or website-content data
# left on disk in an unprotected way?" Three independent layers already keep
# site content in memory only:
#
#   1. browser.privatebrowsing.autostart=true (locked) — PBM keeps cache,
#      IndexedDB, localStorage/sessionStorage, cookies, history, favicons,
#      and CacheStorage in memory only.
#   2. browser.cache.disk.enable=false + disk.capacity=0 + offline.enable=false
#      (locked) — the disk cache backend is hard-disabled even if PBM were
#      somehow bypassed.
#   3. SanitizeOnShutdown locked across all categories in policies.json —
#      final sweep on clean exit.
#
# Other on-disk paths are blocked elsewhere:
#   - History (places.sqlite): places.history.enabled=false + PBM
#   - Form data: DisableFormHistory policy + browser.formfill.enable=false
#   - Session restore: interval=MAX_INT, max_undo=0, resume_from_crash=false
#   - Downloads: patch 004 cancels the channel inside CreateListener before
#     any disk file is opened.
#   - Cookies: SanitizeOnShutdown.Cookies=true + PBM in-memory store
#   - Page thumbnails / shortcut favicons / media cache / service workers:
#     locked off in mozilla.cfg (added the same day as this decision)
#
# A RAM-disk profile would only catch:
#   - Non-content profile metadata (prefs.js counters, extensions.json,
#     compatibility.ini, times.json) — none of which contains page content.
#   - A hypothetical Firefox bug where a PBM code path mistakenly hits disk.
#     Defense-in-depth value is real but small; we already have layered
#     defenses for the content-leak vectors that have historically appeared.
#
# ── What a RAM disk does NOT solve ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OS swap / pagefile / hibernation. A RAM disk on macOS (hdiutil ram://),
# Linux (tmpfs), or Windows (ImDisk) is backed by pageable kernel memory.
# Under memory pressure the OS can write those pages — plus decoded image
# bitmaps in the content-process heap and JIT-compiled JS — to the swap
# file or pagefile. sysdiagnose, WerFault, and kernel crash dumps capture
# the same content. None of that is inside Firefox's control, so wrapping
# the profile in a RAM disk doesn't change the residual exposure.
#
# The correct mitigations are operational, not patch-side:
#   - Require full-disk encryption (FileVault / BitLocker / LUKS) on every
#     deployment host so anything that lands on disk is encrypted at rest
#     under a user- or TPM-bound key.
#   - Suppress hibernation in the OS image, or ensure the hibernation
#     image is encrypted (FDE generally covers this).
#   - On Linux deployments, prefer swapoff or encrypted swap.
#
# These are now documented as deployment prerequisites in README.md.
#
# ── Costs that tipped the trade-off ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   - Windows: needs an external dependency (ImDisk, OSFMount, or similar)
#     and typically admin rights to mount. Conflicts with the "no elevated
#     privileges" deployment assumption.
#   - Linux: tmpfs mount needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN or a user namespace; the
#     newuidmap/newgidmap setup is fragile across distros.
#   - macOS: hdiutil is built-in and the cleanest of the three, but adding
#     mac-only behavior splits the codebase.
#   - Substantial complexity in the binary: RAM-disk setup/teardown,
#     atomic-shutdown handling, crash-recovery cleanup, embedded user.js.
#
# Given the deployment model — controlled environment, no elevated privileges,
# VPN required — requiring FDE on the host is a broader, cheaper, and more
# durable fix than a custom RAM-disk module.
#
# ── Revisit this decision if ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   - Firefox ships a regression that writes page content to disk in PBM.
#   - The deployment target changes to allow elevated privileges.
#   - FDE cannot be enforced on the host (then RAM disk + swapoff becomes
#     the only mitigation, however imperfect).
#   - A swap-encryption or no-swap deployment profile becomes infeasible.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# IMPLEMENTATION GUIDANCE — kept for reference if the decision is reversed
